Why a 4-line config tweak (and not a wholesale removal):

User initially asked to remove the forced memory recall/remember system,
then asked "The hooks can't just be empty?" — choosing the smallest
change that solves the symptom over a full demolition.

The forced behavior came from three things stacked together in
HooksConfig:

  a. Default::default returned non-empty Vecs containing
     "memory_recall" and "memory_remember".
  b. Both fields had serde skip_deserializing, so YAML/JSON values were
     silently dropped and replaced with the defaults — even users
     *trying* to disable hooks couldn't.
  c. The call sites in processor.rs, dispatcher.rs, and reconciler.rs
     gate on has_recall_hook / has_remember_hook, which scan the Vec.

The minimal fix flips a. to empty defaults and removes b. The gates in
c. keep working — they just always return false now. All MemoryHooks
machinery in memory/hooks.rs and the hooks: HooksConfig field on
AgentConfig stay intact. A user on a capable model who edits their
YAML to add `hooks: {before_message: [memory_recall], after_message:
[memory_remember]}` gets the old behavior back.

Why I considered then rejected full removal:

- Deleting hooks.rs (350 lines), ripping out HooksConfig, removing the
  API surface in routes/agents.rs, and the frontend types — all
  reachable from one boolean check returning false. The infrastructure
  isn't actively harmful, just dormant.
- The user's complaint is "small models get confused" — not "this code
  shouldn't exist." Empty defaults solve the actual complaint.
- Leaving the path means re-enabling for Sonnet/GPT-4-class agents is
  a config edit, not a re-implementation.

The earlier commit 4bb12982 (XCLAW-77) had already cleaned up the
hooks' implementation to use no MCP tools — Rust-side memory search
+ LLM synthesis. That work isn't wasted; it's just gated off by
default now.

Tests: existing test_update_config_wake_on_and_hooks now genuinely
round-trips a user-supplied hooks config. Before, it passed only by
coincidence — sending the same values that skip_deserializing was
forcing back. The semantics are stronger, not weaker, after this
change. No new test added because the existing one already covers the
round-trip path.
